@using UBookIt.Web.Rendering
@model IBookingFormView
@{
    var nameError = Model.ErrorFor(BookingFieldIds.Name);
    var emailError = Model.ErrorFor(BookingFieldIds.Email);
}
@*
    Who the booking is for. Identical in both flows and rendered from one place:
    the required-in-text labelling, the per-field aria wiring and the error ids the
    summary links to are exactly the clauses that drift when copied.
*@
<fieldset class="ubookit-details">
    <legend>Your details</legend>

    <div class="ubookit-field">
        <label for="@BookingFieldIds.Name">Name (required)</label>
        <input type="text" id="@BookingFieldIds.Name" name="Name" value="@Model.Name"
               autocomplete="name"
               aria-invalid="@(nameError is not null ? "true" : null)"
               aria-describedby="@(nameError is not null ? "ubookit-name-error" : null)"
               required />
        @if (nameError is not null)
        {
            <span id="ubookit-name-error" class="ubookit-field-error">@nameError</span>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="ubookit-field">
        <label for="@BookingFieldIds.Email">Email (required)</label>
        <input type="email" id="@BookingFieldIds.Email" name="Email" value="@Model.Email"
               autocomplete="email"
               aria-invalid="@(emailError is not null ? "true" : null)"
               aria-describedby="@(emailError is not null ? "ubookit-email-hint ubookit-email-error" : "ubookit-email-hint")"
               required />
        @*
            THE HINT IS BOUND BY THE PRIVACY NOTICE'S REQUIREMENT, not merely consistent with it.
            That requirement governs "any surface of the same form that mentions contact", and this
            is one — the notice and this hint make claims about the same field, on the same screen,
            a few lines apart, so a site where one promises a confirmation and the other does not
            is a site contradicting itself about what it does with an address it is asking for.

            "We'll send your booking confirmation here" was the original wording and was simply
            untrue: the package sent nothing at all, on any site. It became true where a site had
            turned booker messages on AND the host could send them — and then untrue AGAIN when
            approval shipped, because a site with AutoConfirm off sends "we have received your
            booking" at placement, and a booking it declines is never confirmed to anybody.

            So the hint now promises MESSAGES rather than a confirmation, which is true under
            every combination of the two settings. See the note in _PrivacyNotice.cshtml for why
            the alternative — branching on AutoConfirm here as well — was rejected. The condition
            is still SendsBookerEmail, read from the same value the notice reads and the sending
            path is gated on.
        *@
        @if (Model.PrivacyNotice.SendsBookerEmail)
        {
            <span id="ubookit-email-hint" class="ubookit-hint">We'll email you about your booking.</span>
        }
        else
        {
            <span id="ubookit-email-hint" class="ubookit-hint">So this site can contact you about your booking.</span>
        }
        @if (emailError is not null)
        {
            <span id="ubookit-email-error" class="ubookit-field-error">@emailError</span>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="ubookit-field">
        <label for="ubookit-phone">Phone (optional)</label>
        <input type="tel" id="ubookit-phone" name="Phone" value="@Model.Phone"
               autocomplete="tel" />
    </div>

    @* Before the submit control, in the DOM order a screen reader follows, so that what
       happens to the details is read before they are handed over rather than after. *@
    @await Html.PartialAsync("~/Views/Shared/UBookIt/_PrivacyNotice.cshtml", Model)

    <button type="submit" class="ubookit-submit">Book</button>
</fieldset>
